# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, measured 2026-10-10T05:13Z on this service.
#
# `railway up` uploads a working tree, and its archive honours `.gitignore`
# IN ADDITION to this file -- not instead of it. `build_info.json` is
# deliberately gitignored (it is written per deploy; committing it would make
# every tree permanently dirty by one file and make each hash depend on the
# last). So the stamp `stamp_build.py` had just written was EXCLUDED from the
# upload, and the deployment that Railway reported as fine answered:
#
#   /health -> {"build": {"stamped": false, "commit": null,
#                "content_hash": null, "built_at": null,
#                "note": "build_info.json absent -- deploy did not run
#                 stamp_build.py"}}
#
# The code shipped; the identity did not. `deploy.sh` then polled /health for a
# commit and content_hash that could never appear, so the deploy could not
# verify and could not be rolled back to a known build -- the exact failure
# forge-prod hit on 2026-10-05 and closed with this same file. An explicit
# NEGATION here is what actually ships the stamp.
#
# THE OTHER HALF OF THE CONTRACT: every directory excluded below must also be
# in `stamp_build.py`'s SKIP_DIRS, and vice versa. A file uploaded but not
# hashed makes `content_hash` describe something other than what shipped --
# forge-prod measured two different trees sharing one content_hash that way.
# `tests/test_the_build_stamp_actually_ships.py` fails if the two sets drift.
.git/
__pycache__/
.venv/
venv/
node_modules/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.benchmarks/
migrations/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.bak.*
*.backup
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
.DS_Store
junit.xml
pytest.out

# The stamp itself. LAST, because a negation only beats the patterns above it.
!build_info.json
